# PathFuse relay — allow management-overlay control ports + data ports.
# Loaded as a drop-in; install.sh MERGES these into the existing inet filter input
# chain (it does not flush the ruleset).
#
# The rules below are not idempotent: `add` appends without checking for an
# existing copy, so re-running the drop-in duplicates them. Harmless, since all
# of them are `accept`, but prune before re-applying if you want a tidy chain.

# ── OPTIONAL, BY HAND: exempt overlay SSH from a port-22 rate limit ──────────
# Deliberately NOT applied here. Read this before you get locked out.
#
# A hardened relay commonly carries something like
#     tcp dport 22 ct state new limit rate 10/minute accept
# and that limit applies to the management overlay too. Once the token bucket is
# empty a new SYN no longer MATCHES that rule, so it falls through to the chain's
# drop policy: a SILENT HANG, not a refusal. Everything else on the box stays
# reachable, which makes it look like a host fault rather than a firewall rule.
#
# Retrying is what sustains it, though not by "using up" the bucket — an
# over-limit packet matches nothing and consumes no token. The trap is that
# tokens replenish slowly (10/minute is one per 6s) while each retry consumes
# whatever has accrued, and a single ssh attempt emits several SYN
# retransmissions, each its own `ct state new` packet. Retry faster than the
# refill rate and the bucket never gets far enough ahead to carry a handshake.
# Observed: a 20s-spaced loop never recovered. Stop retrying and wait it out.
#
# Why this file does not just add the rule: an exemption has to sit BEFORE the
# rate limiter (or it is never reached once the limit trips) and AFTER any
# access controls you have — e.g. `ip saddr != <admin> tcp dport 22 drop`. This
# drop-in cannot know where either of those lives, and an unconditional accept
# at the head of the chain would precede ALL of them, letting any overlay host
# reach sshd and defeating your source restriction.
#
# So place it yourself, by handle, after your own controls:
#     nft -a list chain inet filter input        # find your port-22 rule handle
#     nft insert rule inet filter input handle <that-handle> \
#         iifname "$overlay_iface" tcp dport 22 accept
# Then persist it in whatever file owns your ruleset. Public port 22 keeps its
# rate limit; only the trusted overlay is exempt.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

add rule inet filter input iifname "$overlay_iface" tcp dport $ports_sbfd_state accept
add rule inet filter input iifname "$overlay_iface" tcp dport $ports_fec_http accept
add rule inet filter input udp dport $ports_engarde accept
